<Layout title="Methods and reproduction"><div class="eyebrow">How the conclusions were reached</div><h1>Four different<br/>evidence questions.</h1><div class="grid two"><section class="card"><span class="number">01</span><h2>Legal change</h2><p>What was prohibited, required or permitted? Read the actual provision and record coverage, exceptions, commencement and court decisions.</p></section><section class="card"><span class="number">02</span><h2>Implementation</h2><p>Did agencies issue guidance, inspect facilities or prosecute cases? Activity demonstrates use of authority, but does not by itself measure welfare.</p></section><section class="card"><span class="number">03</span><h2>Animal outcomes</h2><p>Did exposure, injury, pain, mortality or living conditions change? Record the population, measurement method and follow-up, including what was not counted.</p></section><section class="card"><span class="number">04</span><h2>Causal effect</h2><p>Would those outcomes have been different without the policy? Require a defensible comparison, credible timing and sensitivity to other explanations.</p></section></div>
<div class="prose"><h2>Why this release uses descriptive analysis</h2><p>The retrieved series do not supply a defensible untreated comparison population, stable outcome measurement and adequate pre/post history together. National enactments coincide with state laws, private practices, scientific changes, agency targeting and reporting revisions. An interrupted time series would treat those coincident changes as policy effects unless they could be addressed. Difference-in-differences would need a justified comparison and plausible parallel pre-trends. Neither is established here.</p><p>We therefore transcribe published observations, validate totals and denominators, calculate clearly defined changes, and document alternative explanations. We do not fit causal models, manufacture confidence intervals, or interpret a missing causal estimate as zero impact.</p><h2>Mechanism before model</h2><p>For criminal laws: prohibition → detection → prosecution and deterrence → less exposure to cruelty. For inspections: coverage and detection → corrective action → better care. For research alternatives: validated methods → regulatory acceptance → actual replacement of procedures → less suffering. For trade restrictions: reduced demand and supply → reduced exploitation, subject to displacement. Each policy page identifies where evidence supports this chain and where it stops.</p><h2 id="confidence">Explicit confidence criteria</h2><ul><li><b>High:</b> the exact, bounded claim is directly supported by authoritative text or clearly defined records; material status is checked. This may be a legal or measurement claim only.</li><li><b>Moderate:</b> direct observations exist but selection, incomplete coverage, indirect measurement or independent-verification limits materially constrain interpretation.</li><li><b>Low:</b> evidence is sparse, indirect or materially unresolved. We state the uncertainty and do not turn it into a negative effect claim.</li></ul><p>The separate evidence label—legal only, implementation, observational or causal—describes the kind of evidence. Confidence and impact are different. Official reports are marked as agency-reported unless independent verification is recorded.</p><h2>Counting and denominators</h2><p>Cases, defendants, charges, incidents, inspected entries and individual animals have different units. Selected DOJ releases are a convenience sample and are never summed into national totals. Horse entries may include repeat appearances; multiple noncompliances can arise from one inspection. Animal-use categories distinguish animals held but not used from animals used. Species exclusions and new bird coverage prevent a simple total-use trend from representing all animals.</p><p>FY denotes federal fiscal years, October 1 through September 30. Annual reporting can be revised; data vintages and source-generation dates remain in the ledger. No budget comparisons are presented without a consistent activity definition and inflation adjustment.</p><h2>Attribution and independent opinion</h2><p>We evaluate policies before administrations, record Congress separately from signatures and veto overrides, and attribute later implementation to the institutions that performed it. The site provides no overall weighted score because the measures are not meaningfully commensurate. Any future weighting would encode ethical judgments about severity, species, scale and durability, rather than reveal an objective winner.</p><h2>Research scope</h2><p>The cutoff is October 19, 2026. This is a structured review of named and materially related federal policies, not an exhaustive legal history or a registered systematic review. Primary legal and administrative sources were prioritized; peer-reviewed studies were used for measurement and outcome context. Source access failures and unavailable panels are documented in the <a href={url('coverage/')}>coverage register</a>. Conservation and individual welfare are analyzed separately.</p><h2 id="reproduce">Reproduce and update</h2><pre><code>git clone https://github.com/IamGRootMSE/animal-welfare-policy-evidence.git
cd animal-welfare-policy-evidence
corepack enable
pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
pnpm build
pnpm test
pnpm preview</code></pre><p>The frozen build uses versioned research JSON and requires no API credentials. <code>pnpm sources</code> attempts public source retrieval and records status and SHA-256 hashes; access failures remain failures. Human review is required before updating legal status or transcribed observations. Update the cutoff, review source changes, rerun calculations and tests, and deploy through the repository's GitHub Pages workflow.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/IamGRootMSE/animal-welfare-policy-evidence">Repository, protocol, dictionary, code and licensing →</a></p></div></Layout>
